                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "roswellite":</h3><p><b>Roswellite</b> is the trademarked name for the &#8220;acoustic nanofilm&#8221; material developed by Robert Crowley of Soundwave Research Laboratories. It is a &#8220;superelastic, paramagnetic&#8221; composite material with high tensile strength, approximately equal conductivity to elemental aluminum, great elasticity, and the ability to retain its shape memory even after exposure to deformation due to high sound pressure levels or even electrical current.</p><p>Read more about <a href="http://microphonium.blogspot.com/2008/02/roswellite-faq-frequently-asked.html">Roswellite</a>.</p><p>Read more about the process of <a href="http://microphonium.blogspot.com/2006/09/fire-and-soot.html">growing carbon nanotubes</a> &#8212; thought to be an integral process in the manufacture of Roswellite.</p><p>Roswellite transducers can be found in selected microphones from Crowley and Tripp, and from Shure (which acquired the rights to the Crowley and Tripp product line in 2009).</p>
